 \section{Introduction}
-Mixture-of-experts (MoE) models scale parameter capacity through sparse expert activation~\cite{shazeer,switch,mixtral,deepseekmoe}. For example, Mixtral selects two of eight experts in each layer. A single-GPU deployment can use high-bandwidth memory (HBM), host memory, and a hard disk drive (HDD) to hold that pool. The selected expert's location then determines its delivery path. HBM residents execute in place; host residents require a host-to-device (H2D) copy; cold experts require an HDD read followed by H2D. The next layer consumes the current layer's output, so an exposed read also delays downstream computation. These accesses recur at successive MoE layers during generation. Prefill can reuse one fetched expert across prompt rows, and decoding can reuse its weights across output tokens. Retaining the corresponding cache state avoids fetching those weights again. The placement problem is therefore tied to the request sequence, rather than to model loading alone. On shared servers, a serving task manages its expert allocation alongside other jobs and model instances. We study how to distribute that allocation across layers and preserve the resulting reuse during inference. All MoE layers share the HBM and host allowances. Increasing one layer's allocation leaves fewer slots for others. The value of another slot depends on the demand already covered in that layer. Placement therefore couples the layers' local request distributions to a shared storage allocation.
